{"purpose":"Explain the self-referential bootstrap: the eka project uses EKA itself as its development knowledge system, so humans and agents understand the old-line/tool vs new-line/target distinction.","content":"The eka project builds the next-generation EKA ecosystem (eka-standard, eka-core, eka-cli, eka-mcp). Its own development uses EKA itself — the installed `eka` binary (the old line) is the knowledge management system that tracks this work. This is self-referential: Old EKA = tooling + development knowledge; New EKA = the architecture and implementation being built. Both are one codebase lineage; when the new line matures it replaces the old line (self-hosting). Do not conflate 'EKA the tool' (the binary you are running) with 'EKA the target' (the repositories you are working on). The tool is the vehicle; the target is the deliverable."}